---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: scripts-ci-governance
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  Bash entrypoints invoked by the Jenkins pipelines and — critically — by the deploy container
  (render gates, mount verification, conductor-config validation). One class of drift is
  gate-surfaced here: a script that runs in the deploy container reaches for an interpreter or
  library that container does not have (PyYAML/python/pip). The deploy container is bash+coreutils
  ONLY; edge #1183 failed all seven human deploys on a single PyYAML import — the validator that was
  supposed to guard defect #1 could not run in its own container (museum defect class #5). The
  requirement is written into each deploy script's header; this rule surfaces it at edit time.
rules:
  - id: deploy-scripts-bash-coreutils-only
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.sh", contains-any: ["python", "PyYAML", "pip install", "pip3", "import yaml", "env python"] }
    dedupe-of: "each deploy script's own header requirement (bash + coreutils only), scarred by edge #1183"
    why: >
      INVARIANT — deploy-container scripts are bash + coreutils ONLY. The render/deploy container
      has no python, no pip, no PyYAML — a script that imports one fails BEFORE `kubectl apply`,
      silently pinning the fleet on pre-fix builds (edge #1183 failed all 7 human deploys on a
      PyYAML import; it made the fixes it was gating look inert). Parse YAML/JSON with bash +
      coreutils (grep/sed/awk), not a python one-liner. And VERIFY where it RUNS, not just locally:
      a CI tool negative-tested outside its runtime is itself an unverified claim — the same class
      it polices. If a build-only script (e.g. cargo-publish-*.sh) legitimately needs a richer
      toolchain, confirm it never runs on the deploy path before proceeding.
  - id: long-wait-default-cites-its-price
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.sh", contains-any: ["_SECS:-"] }
    dedupe-of: "the habit that prices pipeline wall clock: genesis/orchestrator/.epr-meta/push-delivers-within-budget.habit.md (bounds stage-wallclock-ceiling@1, delivery-cost-ceiling@1 in .claude/epr-meta/measures.yaml)"
    why: >
      A `*_SECS:-<n>` default over 900 s is pipeline wall clock someone pays on every run. Beside
      it, in a comment, name the habit that prices it (push-delivers-within-budget, or the habit
      whose measure the wait serves) and why the wait belongs in THIS pipeline. Evidence-ladder spec
      §8 (genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-10-evidence-ladder-push-left-design.md): a2d1d0975
      set STAGE_CELL_READY_BUDGET_SECS=7200 in stage-spa-blob.sh, passed six reviews of the bash and
      none of the placement, and app #1719–#1725 then spent ≈12 pipeline-hours waiting out the
      fleet's post-roll window and delivered nothing. Prefer a precondition that refuses in seconds
      over a wait. A default at or under 900 s needs nothing — this advisory fires on any default
      because the gate matches substrings, not numbers.
cites:
  - substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc
  - evidence-ladder-push-left
