# scripts/ci — pipeline + deploy-container bash entrypoints

Bash scripts called by the Jenkins pipelines (`sh "bash '${WORKSPACE}/scripts/ci/<name>.sh' …"`)
and by the deploy container. This manifest CLAIMS full responsibility for the subtree
(`covers: subtree`).

## Deploy-container runtime invariant (edit-time advisory)

**The deploy container is bash + coreutils only — no python, no pip, no PyYAML.** A deploy-time
script (the render gates, `validate-conductor-config.sh`, `verify-epr-mount.sh`, `stage-spa-blob.sh`)
that reaches for a python interpreter or library dies before `kubectl apply` runs, and — because it
runs *before* the apply — it silently pins the whole fleet on the pre-fix build. This is defect
class #5 of the five-defect convergence arc: *CI tooling verified outside its runtime is itself an
unverified claim.* Edge #1183 is the scar: one `import yaml` failed all seven human deploys and made
the defect-#2/#3 fixes it was gating look inert.

The `deploy-scripts-bash-coreutils-only` rule fires as an `inject` advisory when a `.sh` write in
this subtree reaches for python/pip/PyYAML. It names no validator — the real check is running the
script in the container it deploys from, which is the very discipline the rule teaches, and a
`validator:` key naming a detector the resolver does not implement would advertise enforcement the
rule does not serve (the phantom key was dropped 2026-08-02, plan task P4.1). It surfaces the
constraint at edit time. Two disciplines the advisory
teaches: parse config with bash+coreutils, and TEST IN THE CONTAINER, not just on the host. Note the
subtree also holds build-only scripts (e.g. `cargo-publish-*.sh`) that legitimately use a fuller
toolchain because they never run on the deploy path — the rule is content-scoped to the python class
so it does not toll them.

## Long wait defaults are priced (edit-time advisory)

A `*_SECS:-<n>` default above 900 s is paid on every run, so it must say which habit prices it —
evidence-ladder spec §8 is the scar: `STAGE_CELL_READY_BUDGET_SECS=7200` (a2d1d0975) turned the
App pipeline into a two-hour wait for the edge fleet's post-roll window, and app #1719–#1725
spent ≈12 pipeline-hours delivering nothing while every review read the bash and none the
placement. The `long-wait-default-cites-its-price` rule is an `inject` advisory on any `.sh`
write carrying a `_SECS:-` default. The 900 s threshold is authoritative but NOT gate-expressible
in v1 (`contains-any` matches substrings, not numbers), so the author judges it: under 900 s,
ignore the advisory; over it, add a comment naming the habit (usually
`push-delivers-within-budget`) and why the wait belongs in this pipeline rather than as a
precondition that refuses in seconds. The cost itself is measured, not asserted:
`stage-wallclock@1` and `delivery-cost@1`, folded by `ci-harvest.py` from
`genesis/orchestrator/delivery-series.mjs --stages`.

See: `genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/2026-07-12-substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc.md`
